{#- How a bundle is handed to the workshop. Included mid-sentence by every skill
   that submits a local bundle, and whole by the language reference's "Template
   files" section, so it strips its own trailing newline (the closing comment
   below). It has a folder form and a listing form.

   The folder form, the default, passes the bundle directory as `method_dir`
   (`pipelex-mcp`, SPEC.md, "The method folder"): the workshop reads the
   folder's `.mthds` files and the template files their `PipeDocGen` steps name,
   sends them as `files` would, and lists them in `files_sent`, so no template a
   hand-made list forgets is left out and no file slips in unseen. Which files
   belong to the folder is the workshop's rule, and it reads the `.gitignore`
   files inside the folder, which is how a saved run stays out of the method:
   `mthds_download_artifacts` writes a `.gitignore` of `*` into the topmost
   directory it creates, and `/pipelex-run` writes one into a `runs/` made
   before the workshop did. The folder is the one source on the tooling tools
   and pairs with a bare `method_id` on `mthds_run`, as `files` did, so a skill
   says `method_dir` where it said `files` and nothing else changes.

   The listing form (`validate_call_folder = false`) is the convention the
   folder replaced, kept where a skill states the file set itself:
   `pipelex-catalog`, since `mthds_save_method` takes no folder, its `files` and
   `python` being what the catalog stores; `pipelex-integrate`, since the set it
   submits is the set its generated call site loads at runtime, which is the
   call site's to define; and the language reference, whose fallback for a
   workshop older than the folder form lists the files. The file set is every
   `.mthds` file beneath the bundle directory except `runs/`, where
   `/pipelex-run` saves a completed run's artifacts, and every template file a
   `PipeDocGen` step names in its `template_file`; the workshop keys each
   template by the step naming it and refuses one no step names, which is why
   the set is the named files and not a glob of template suffixes, which would
   sweep in the `method-graph.html` page `mthds_validate` writes beside the
   bundle. Its parameters apply to it alone. `validate_call_file_set` is false
   in `pipelex-integrate`, which states its own set. `validate_call_templates`
   is false in `pipelex-catalog`, since the catalog stores `.mthds` and `.py`
   files only. `validate_call_root_first` is false in `pipelex-catalog`, whose
   save step states the order with its own reason: the workshop reads every
   `{ path }` item from at or under the directory of the first one (SPEC.md,
   "Path trust boundary"), so the root `.mthds` file goes first.
   `validate_call_inline` is what the last sentence says of the inline form;
   the catalog sets it, because `mthds_save_method` writes `pipelex-method.json`
   only beside a `{ path }` root file, so for a save the inline form is the one
   that leaves a new method unlinked.

   Both forms name the same bound: the local workshop reads a folder or a
   `{ path }` item only inside its own working directory, an absolute path
   included (`src/files.ts`), so the sentence names the refusal and its cure
   rather than promising that any absolute path works. And both name the same
   fallback: a bundle the workshop cannot read goes inline as `files`, which a
   templated one cannot, since a template file has no inline form, so a
   templated bundle out of reach is a stop and the relaunch. The folder form
   says which files go inline, since no folder rule picks them there: the
   listing's set, the `.mthds` files outside `runs/`, root file first, as
   `{content, uri}`, where an inline `uri` is a provenance label the workshop
   reads nothing from, so the sentence leaves its value to the caller. The
   listing form's `uri: <path relative to the bundle dir>` is that label's
   convention. It does not call `method_dir` absolute: a
   relative one resolves against the workshop's working directory, as a
   `{ path }` does.

   The folder form's last sentence is the pointer to the language reference's
   fallback for a workshop older than the folder form, which strips an argument
   it does not know and answers that no method was given: a branch, so the
   listing it falls back to, the refresh it names and the run's pairing it
   forbids live in the reference, read before the next call. Why the folder,
   and why the `.gitignore` in `runs/`, is rationale and lives in
   `docs/decisions.md`. -#}
{%- if validate_call_folder | default(true) %}Pass the bundle directory as `method_dir`: the workshop sends its `.mthds` files and the templates their steps name, listed in `files_sent`. It refuses one outside **its own** working directory; relaunching the harness from a directory holding the bundle cures that. Inline `files` (the bundle's `.mthds` files outside `runs/`, root file first, as `{content, uri}`) are the fallback for a bundle with no template file; **for a templated one, stop and ask for that relaunch**. A workshop whose tools lack `method_dir`, or answer it *Provide MTHDS files*, is older: read [the reference]({{ shared_dir }}/writing-mthds.md#template-files) first.
{%- else %}{% set validate_call_with_templates = validate_call_templates | default(true) %}{% if validate_call_file_set | default(true) %}Submit every `.mthds` file beneath the bundle directory **except anything under a `runs/` directory**{% if validate_call_with_templates %}, and every template file a `PipeDocGen` step names{% endif %}. {% endif %}Prefer the path form `{path: <absolute path>}`{% if validate_call_root_first | default(true) %}, **the root `.mthds` file first**, as nothing above its directory is read{% endif %}. The workshop refuses a path outside **its own** working directory, where the harness launched it; relaunching the harness from a directory holding the bundle cures that. Inline `{content, uri: <path relative to the bundle dir>}` {{ validate_call_inline | default("is the fallback for a bundle with no template file; **for a templated one, stop and ask for that relaunch**.") }}{% endif %}
{#- -#}
